    <h2>Why Generators Became the Growth Job</h2>

    <p>Backup power is the electrical work Hartselle homeowners ask about most now, and it splits into two very different jobs. The honest starting point most installers skip: a whole-home standby generator that runs everything automatically is a $9,000-to-$16,000 project, and most homeowners do not need it. The storms here take the power out a handful of times a year, not weekly, and a 7,500-watt portable generator wired to the panel through an interlock kit will carry the fridge, a heat source, and the essential circuits for a fraction of the cost. The standby unit earns its price if you are on a well, run medical equipment, or simply refuse to lose power — a real preference in a preparedness-minded town, just not a universal need.</p>

    <p>The part that is not optional is how the generator ties into the house. This is the most dangerous mistake in residential electrical: a homeowner backfeeds a portable generator through a dryer outlet with a double-male suicide cord. That energizes the utility lines outside — it can kill a lineman restoring power on Nance Ford Road — and it defeats every protection in the panel. The safe method is a transfer switch or interlock that makes it physically impossible to feed the grid and the generator at once. It is a few hundred dollars and a couple of hours, and no competent electrician installs a generator any other way.</p>

    <h2>The Panels Hiding in Older Homes</h2>

    <p>The steady work under the generator boom is panels. Plenty of Hartselle's older homes — the mid-century blocks and neighborhoods like Cain Park — still run 100-amp service that was plenty in 1970 and is stretched thin the moment a homeowner adds central air, a hot tub, an EV charger, or a shop out back. Upgrading to 200-amp service is the fix, and it is often bundled with a generator install because both touch the panel anyway.</p>

    <p>The more urgent panel issue is the brand, and this is a genuine safety matter, not a sales pitch. Federal Pacific Stab-Lok and Zinsco panels were installed across the country for decades and are now documented to sometimes fail to trip under overload — a breaker that does not break is a fire waiting for a bad circuit. An electrician who has opened a few hundred panels recognizes these on sight. If your home has one, replacing it is worth doing regardless of capacity. The same era of homes sometimes hides aluminum branch wiring, which is safe only when its connections are properly pigtailed with the right connectors — another thing invisible behind a cover plate until someone who knows what they are looking at pulls it.</p>

    <h2>Service Beyond the City Limits</h2>

    <p>Most Hartselle electricians work a radius well past the city, and the generator and panel work follows the same map — out west toward Trinity, east to Somerville, and through the rural stretches where a well pump makes a power outage a water outage too. On those rural lots, backup power stops being a convenience and becomes the reason the household has water when the grid is down, which is exactly why the standby-generator conversation lands differently a few miles out of town than it does on a city lot two blocks from Cain Park.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Electrical Work Costs in Hartselle</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, and what moves them:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Service call / diagnostic</strong> — about $90 to $160 to show up and find the fault.</li>
      <li><strong>100-to-200-amp panel upgrade</strong> — roughly $1,800 to $4,500, higher if the meter base and service entrance are replaced too.</li>
      <li><strong>Generator interlock kit</strong> — a few hundred dollars installed, the safe way to run a portable.</li>
      <li><strong>Whole-home standby generator</strong> — about $9,000 to $16,000 with the automatic transfer switch and gas hookup.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Rewiring outdated circuits is the one job nobody quotes cleanly up front, because the electrician cannot see what is behind the walls until the work opens them. A good one gives you a per-circuit price and a realistic range rather than a single confident number.</p>

    <h2>What Waits and What Doesn't</h2>

    <p>Panel safety issues do not wait — a Federal Pacific or Zinsco panel, warm breakers, or a burning smell is a call-today situation, not a someday project. Generators, by contrast, reward planning: install one in the calm of fall, not in the scramble after a storm has already taken the power and every installer within fifty miles is booked three weeks out.</p>

    <h2>Common Electrical Questions in Hartselle</h2>

    <p><strong>Standby generator or portable?</strong></p>
    <p>Most homes do fine with a portable through an interlock kit. Standby makes sense on a well, with medical needs, or if you refuse to lose power at all.</p>

    <p><strong>Can I run my house off a dryer outlet?</strong></p>
    <p>No. A suicide cord can kill a lineman and bypasses every panel safety. Use a transfer switch or interlock — it is cheap and it is the law.</p>

    <p><strong>Does my panel need replacing?</strong></p>
    <p>If it is 100-amp and you are adding load, upgrade to 200. If it is a Federal Pacific or Zinsco, replace it for safety regardless.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>Panel upgrades $1,800 to $4,500, interlocks a few hundred, whole-home standby generators $9,000 to $16,000.</p>

